Two systems, both private company code, described in architecture only. Grouped because they
share a problem: a model needs access to more sources, or more tools, than you can usefully put
in front of it at once.

## A research assistant that has to cite

Retrieval over Singapore tax and legal material — statutes, judgments, commentaries and
tax-authority guidance — answering with citations across several jurisdictions.

For legal research an uncited answer is worse than no answer: it cannot be checked and it reads
as authoritative. That constraint shaped the retrieval, not just the output format.

**Sources are not interchangeable, so retrieval was not flat.** A statute, a judgment and a
practitioner commentary differ in authority and in how they are cited. Collapsing them into one
index loses that, so each source got its own namespace with its own retrieval depth, reranking
behaviour, similarity cutoff and metadata filters, with a selection step choosing among them.

**Statute lookup turned out to be two different problems:**

<Flow steps={[
  { label: 'Question', detail: 'Routed by what is actually being asked' },
  { label: '"What does section 10(1)(b) say?"', detail: 'Exact retrieval. The user has named the thing — similarity search is the wrong tool, because a near-miss returns a neighbouring subsection with quiet confidence. Graph traversal over the legislation' },
  { label: '"What provision covers this situation?"', detail: 'Genuinely a search problem. Vector retrieval over statute content' },
  { label: 'Case law: whole judgments, not chunks', detail: 'A holding does not survive being cut into fragments' },
  { label: 'Cited answer', detail: 'Multi-source citation engine, with a retry instruction to try another source rather than conclude nothing exists', kind: 'gate' }
]} />

Models were self-hosted at this stage — open-weight models served locally with an embedding and
reranking service alongside — chosen for cost control and for keeping client material in-house,
with a backup model and retry when a service was unavailable. Each engine was containerised
separately and ran on GPU hosts.

**Where my work ends.** I took this through its first three milestones as founding engineer. The
production platform that replaced it — a different retrieval stack, hosted models, a deep
research mode, and the evaluation suite with its measured benchmarks — was built by a colleague
after I moved off the project. Those numbers are not mine to present, and I have no measured
results from my own period on it.

## A voice agent with too many tools

A speech-to-speech assistant over a set of integrations — calendar, mail, messaging and social
platforms — exposed through MCP servers.

The naive approach hands the model every tool from every server. That fails twice over: the tool
list crowds the context, and a realtime model facing dozens of similar tool names picks the wrong
one with some regularity.

<Flow steps={[
  { label: 'Speech in', detail: 'Realtime model, server-side voice activity detection, barge-in so the user can interrupt mid-reply' },
  { label: 'mcp_search', detail: 'The model searches for a capability instead of being handed a directory. Query rewriting plus a vector router over the tool catalogue returns names and descriptions only', kind: 'model' },
  { label: 'get_mcp_tool_details', detail: 'Schema fetched for one candidate, on demand' },
  { label: 'mcp_call', detail: 'The call itself. Output is summarised before re-entering the conversation — a raw API response spends context the voice loop needs' },
  { label: 'Self-correction', detail: 'The model sometimes skips the search and guesses a tool name; the gateway detects that and re-injects guidance rather than returning a bare error', kind: 'gate' }
]} note="Three meta-tools in front of a router, rather than the full inventory in the prompt." />

The auth boundary rejects API keys passed in the query string when running in production — one of
the few places here with a real unit test behind it, because that is exactly the kind of
convenience that survives into production by accident.

The gateway was migrated between realtime providers during development, which is why
provider-specific handling sits behind an internal interface. An earlier spike evaluated a
different speech-to-speech model with a hand-rolled bidirectional event protocol; it stayed a
prototype, used push-to-talk, and had no interruption handling.

**No latency figures.** The gateway has jitter-buffer and turn-timing instrumentation and I used
it while building, but no results were recorded anywhere I can cite. I would rather say that than
reconstruct plausible numbers after the fact.

## Stack

Python, LlamaIndex, Neo4j, Pinecone, Ollama, vLLM, FastAPI. TypeScript, Node, OpenAI Realtime,
MCP. Docker throughout.
